name 'pl_sandbox_aif_2_U2-DOM-7_2026'
title 'title'
description 'description here'

markdown <<MARKDOWN
<h1>From Event → State → Response to the DOM</h1>

<p style="font-size:14pt;">
On the last page, we saw the pattern that powers interactivity:
<strong>Event → State → Response</strong>.
</p>

<p style="font-size:14pt;">
But how does JavaScript actually <em>reach into a webpage</em> to make those changes happen?
That’s where the <strong>DOM</strong> comes in.
</p>

<hr>

<h2>From HTML to Interactivity: 3 Steps</h2>

<div style="display:flex; align-items:stretch; justify-content:center; gap:14px; flex-wrap:wrap; margin:18px 0;">

  <div style="background:#0093a4; color:white; border-radius:10px; padding:14px 18px; min-width:170px; text-align:center;">
    <div style="font-size:18pt; font-weight:bold;">1️⃣ Select</div>
    <div style="font-size:14pt; margin-top:6px;">
      Choose an element from the page
    </div>
  </div>

  <div style="font-size:26pt; font-weight:bold; color:#0093a4; display:flex; align-items:center;">→</div>

  <div style="background:#0093a4; color:white; border-radius:10px; padding:14px 18px; min-width:170px; text-align:center;">
    <div style="font-size:18pt; font-weight:bold;">2️⃣ Listen</div>
    <div style="font-size:14pt; margin-top:6px;">
      Wait for a user event (click, type, hover)
    </div>
  </div>

  <div style="font-size:26pt; font-weight:bold; color:#0093a4; display:flex; align-items:center;">→</div>

  <div style="background:#0093a4; color:white; border-radius:10px; padding:14px 18px; min-width:170px; text-align:center;">
    <div style="font-size:18pt; font-weight:bold;">3️⃣ Update</div>
    <div style="font-size:14pt; margin-top:6px;">
      Change content, style, or data
    </div>
  </div>

</div>

<p style="font-size:14pt;">
Those three steps are how we turn static HTML into something dynamic.
</p>

<hr>

<h2>Understanding the DOM</h2>

<p style="font-size:14pt;">
The <strong>DOM (Document Object Model)</strong> is the system that makes those three steps possible.
Think of the DOM like a <strong>live blueprint of your webpage</strong>.
If HTML is the building plan and CSS is the paint and decor, the DOM is the 
<strong>labeled wiring system inside the walls</strong> that lets JavaScript flip switches and make things happen.
</p>

<img src="https://images.code.org/d77aa4ddfe768eb2e16d6072e8ac323f-image-1761850949297.png" 
     alt="simple html map going from head to id of green button" 
     width="350px" 
     style="border-radius:10px; float:left; margin: 10px 30px 10px 40px;">

<div style="margin-left:420px;">

  <p style="font-size:14pt;">
  The DOM turns every HTML element (text, images, buttons) into an <strong>object</strong> that JavaScript can:
  </p>

  <ul style="padding-left:20px;">
    <li style="font-size:14pt;">Select</li>
    <li style="font-size:14pt;">Listen to</li>
    <li style="font-size:14pt;">Modify</li>
  </ul>

  <p style="font-size:14pt;">
  It organizes the page in a tree structure: the document at the top, 
  then branches like <code>head</code> and <code>body</code>, and finally individual elements inside them.
  </p>

</div>

<div style="clear:both;"></div>

<br>

<p style="font-size:14pt;">
When JavaScript interacts with the DOM, <strong>structure (HTML), style (CSS), and behavior (JavaScript)</strong> work together as one unified, dynamic system.
</p>

<hr>

<h2>Next Up</h2>

<p style="font-size:14pt;">
Now let’s see what this looks like in actual code.
</p>

MARKDOWN
